 "method": "Each valid 80 m sample is water if HH backscatter (and HV, when it separates water from land on that pass) is below a threshold trained per pass on the pooled samples of its frames: the midpoint between the 25th percentile of samples in JRC persistent-water cells (occurrence >= 90%) and the 5th percentile of samples in never-water cells (0%), clamped HH (-20.0, -11.0) dB / HV (-28.0, -16.0) dB; passes whose water/land separation is < 2.0 dB are rejected. A ~0.55 km cell is water when >= 50% of its valid samples are water; cells with < 30% valid samples are no-data. Frames of one pass are mosaicked. Possible inundated vegetation (L-band double bounce): samples with HH >= -3.0 dB and HH-HV >= 7.0 dB; a cell is labelled so when >= 50% of its samples are. Cells bright in >= 90% of >= 5 passes are treated as persistent structures (urban, bridges) and excluded.",
 "limitations": "Inundated-vegetation thresholds are fixed literature-style values, not trained on local ground truth; urban areas and other double-bounce structures can still be confused with flooded vegetation, and some flooded vegetation is not bright enough to be detected. Wind-roughened water can be missed; radar shadow and very smooth surfaces can be mistaken for water. Not validated against ground truth.",
